{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Why this ticket exists\n\nEpic 6202 stories declared gate-scratch/docker-storage infrastructure, but earlier acceptance evidence proved declarations and CI only, not live provisioning. This ticket owns the operator-approved maintenance needed to reconcile live AWS/on-box state and to add a recurring declared-vs-provisioned check so this shape cannot recur silently.\n\n## Current fresh plan envelope (2026-09-07)\n\nFresh `terraform plan` from current Gerrit main after antelope+salamander applies is authoritative for the apply stop condition:\n\n- `update aws_instance.gerrit` (`replace=[]`) — `user_data` hash convergence only, because current `user_data.sh` renders the gate-scratch volume id/mount into first-boot configuration.\n- `0 add`, `0 destroy`, `0 replace`.\n\nPreviously cited envelopes with gate-scratch volume/alarm creates are historical and are superseded by this fresh plan: those Terraform resources now exist in state/AWS. Stop without applying if a fresh plan shows any destroy, replacement, or non-no-op resource outside `aws_instance.gerrit` user_data convergence and any explicitly approved gate-scratch/docker alarm resource.\n\n## Live work this ticket still owns\n\n1. Verify the gate-scratch EBS volume exists, is attached, formatted, mounted at the configured mount point, and that both marker files are on the mounted volume rather than the root filesystem.\n2. Verify `/var/gerrit` remains mounted after the instance cycle. `user_data.sh` now does assert `/var/gerrit` (`infra/terraform/user_data.sh:192-193`) and FATALs if that mount is not in service; the remaining risk is that a stop/start does not re-run first-boot cloud-init, so this ticket independently verifies the live mount after the cycle.\n3. Verify all five alarms exist and are SNS-wired: the two gate-scratch alarms plus `docker_storage_cap_high` (`infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf:474`), `docker_buildkit_cache_high` (`infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf:523`), and `docker_unaccounted_bytes` (`infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf:574`).\n4. Restart Docker in the quiet window and prove `docker-storage-cap.sh --install` reports the BuildKit policy in effect; SIGHUP/reload is not sufficient for `builder.gc`.\n5. Land the declared-vs-provisioned check through Gerrit by reusing/extending the existing prior art `scripts/assert_volumes_in_service.py` from bug dcc3. That script already joins Terraform `aws_volume_attachment` data against live `lsblk -P -o NAME,SERIAL,MOUNTPOINT` output and classifies volumes as in-service / NOT-IN-SERVICE / UNKNOWN. The code change must extend that existing implementation to cover the gate-scratch volume/mount and, because this ticket requires recurring signal rather than only an apply-time one-shot, wire its classified outcome into the observability metric emission path. A state-only check is explicitly insufficient because it can miss the S1 mode where Terraform declaration/state and live mount reality diverge.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 - The gate-scratch volume exists in AWS and in Terraform state, and is attached. Evidence: `describe-volumes` shows it attached to the instance.\n- [ ] AC2 - It is formatted, mounted at the configured mount point, and BOTH markers exist with the proof marker verifiably ON THE VOLUME (it disappears on unmount and returns on remount). `rebar/host:gate_scratch_mounted` publishes **1**.\n- [ ] AC3 - `/var/gerrit` is verified mounted after the instance cycle. Current `user_data.sh` does assert `/var/gerrit` and FATAL on failure; because stop/start does not re-run first-boot cloud-init, live post-cycle verification is still required. Gerrit responding is NOT evidence — it responds either way.\n- [ ] AC4 - All five alarms (2 gate-scratch + 3 docker-storage) exist in AWS with `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` and SNS wiring, and their states are recorded.\n- [ ] AC5 - `systemctl restart docker` has been performed in a quiet window and `docker-storage-cap.sh --install` subsequently reports the policy IN EFFECT. Until this, the BuildKit cap is not in force.\n- [ ] AC6 - A runtime declared-vs-provisioned check exists and has landed through Gerrit by reusing/extending `scripts/assert_volumes_in_service.py` (bug dcc3): its Terraform `aws_volume_attachment` vs live `lsblk -P -o NAME,SERIAL,MOUNTPOINT` join/classification covers gate-scratch, and the recurring observability path emits a metric from that outcome so declaration/state and live mounts cannot diverge silently.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nNot changing any story's ACs retroactively. Not applying Terraform outside operator approval. Not relying on Terraform state alone as proof of live provisioning.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `terraform plan` in `infra/terraform` must match the fresh envelope above before apply.\n- AWS and on-box checks must prove the EBS volume exists, is attached, mounted at the configured gate-scratch path, and contains the markers on the mounted volume.\n- Verify `/var/gerrit` remains mounted after the instance cycle.\n- Verify all relevant CloudWatch alarms exist with `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` and SNS actions.\n- After `systemctl restart docker`, run the installed docker-storage cap check and record that the policy is in effect.\n- The declared-vs-provisioned check is a code change that must pass local verification, Gerrit LLM-Review+1, Gerrit Verified+1, and submit before this ticket closes.\n\n## Operator Approval, Ordering, and Abort Conditions\n\nOperator approval was recorded on 2026-09-06 for the Terraform apply, on-box gate-scratch mount, daemon restart, and the declared-vs-provisioned code check. Execute this as an operator-approved maintenance ticket. Pause `rebar-autodeploy.timer` before any apply or manual restart step and re-enable it only after health checks pass. Stop if observed downtime would exceed about 15 minutes.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788784926841241001,"uuid":"6a86f7cd-ecd0-48a1-9415-dbf9883c8de2"}